21.4.4 Fig 4.4 Bar charts
Source: Lattice Fig 4.4. Data: Death rates in Virginia (1940), R datasets package VADeaths (Molyneaux et al. 1947); licence: Part of R (GPL-2 | GPL-3).

(plot/render-image
 {:data va-deaths
  :mapping {:x :rate :y :age :facet :group}
  :charts [(chart/bar)]
  :facets {:columns 1}
  :labels {:title "Death Rates in Virginia - 1940" :x "Rate (per 1000)" :y nil}}
 {:width 450 :height 750})






21.4.5 Fig 4.5 Proportions as stacked bars
Reasons for taking a postdoctoral position, as proportions of each field (:position :normalize), in R’s order.
Source: Lattice Fig 4.5. Data: Reasons for taking a postdoc, by field, R latticeExtra package postdoc (NSF Survey of Doctorate Recipients 1999); licence: GPL (&gt;= 2) (latticeExtra).

(plot/render-image
 {:data postdoc
  :mapping {:x :count :y :field :color :reason}
  :scales {:color {:domain (nu/levels :postdoc :reason)}}
  :charts [(chart/bar {:position :normalize})]
  :labels {:x "Proportion" :y nil}}
 {:width 900 :height 450})






21.4.6 Fig 4.6 Proportions as dot plots
Source: Lattice Fig 4.6. Data: Reasons for taking a postdoc, by field, R latticeExtra package postdoc (NSF Survey of Doctorate Recipients 1999); licence: GPL (&gt;= 2) (latticeExtra).

(def postdoc-proportions
  "postdoc counts as proportions of each field's total."
  (let [totals (update-vals (group-by :field postdoc) #(reduce + (map :count %)))]
    (mapv #(assoc % :proportion (/ (double (:count %)) (double (totals (:field %))))) postdoc)))


(plot/render-image
 {:data postdoc-proportions
  :mapping {:x :proportion :y :field :facet :reason}
  :charts [(chart/scatter)]
  :facets {:columns 3}
  :labels {:x "Proportion" :y nil}}
 {:width 1000 :height 550})






21.4.7 Fig 4.7 Ordered panels and levels
Panels ordered by their median proportion, the fields within each panel by their proportion (free y scales).
Source: Lattice Fig 4.7. Data: Reasons for taking a postdoc, by field, R latticeExtra package postdoc (NSF Survey of Doctorate Recipients 1999); licence: GPL (&gt;= 2) (latticeExtra).

(plot/render-image
 {:data postdoc-proportions
  :mapping {:x :proportion :y :field :facet :reason}
  :scales {:facet {:order-by #(stats/median (map :proportion %)) :descending true}
           :y {:order-by #(stats/mean (map :proportion %))}}
  :charts [(chart/scatter)]
  :facets {:columns 1 :scales {:y :free}}
  :labels {:x "Proportion" :y nil}}
 {:width 600 :height 800})






21.4.8 Fig 4.8 Frequencies as spikes
How many candidates have each average GCSE score, by gender.
Source: Lattice Fig 4.8. Data: A-level chemistry scores, England 1997, R mlmRev package Chem97 (Yang et al. 2002); licence: GPL (&gt;= 2) (mlmRev).

(plot/render-image
 {:data (for [[[score gender] n] (frequencies (map (juxt :gcsescore :gender) chem97))]
          {:gcsescore score :gender gender :freq n})
  :mapping {:x :gcsescore :y :freq :facet :gender}
  :charts [(chart/segment {:yend 0})]
  :facets {:columns 1}
  :labels {:x "Average GCSE Score" :y "Freq"}}
 {:width 800 :height 500})






21.4.9 Fig 4.9 Bar chart of counts
Source: Lattice Fig 4.9. Data: A-level chemistry scores, England 1997, R mlmRev package Chem97 (Yang et al. 2002); licence: GPL (&gt;= 2) (mlmRev).

